1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:02,731
Changi Prison, 1936 to 2004:

2
00:00:02,811 --> 00:00:06,625
The Gaol Whose Wall and Gate Were Kept

3
00:00:06,625 --> 00:00:11,244
Along Upper Changi Road North
stands a fragment of a prison:

4
00:00:11,324 --> 00:00:16,334
an entrance gate, 180 metres of
high wall and two corner turrets,

5
00:00:16,414 --> 00:00:20,800
kept when everything behind
them was demolished in 2004.

6
00:00:20,800 --> 00:00:25,021
The fragment is a national
monument, gazetted in 2016,

7
00:00:25,101 --> 00:00:31,075
but it stands inside a restricted area
and almost nobody can walk up to it.

8
00:00:31,075 --> 00:00:33,417
The gaol it belonged to was, in turn,

9
00:00:33,497 --> 00:00:38,589
Singapore's most modern prison, an
internment camp for the colony's own rulers,

10
00:00:38,669 --> 00:00:41,928
the most crowded prisoner-of-war
cage in Singapore,

11
00:00:42,008 --> 00:00:45,267
the place where Japanese
war criminals were hanged,

12
00:00:45,347 --> 00:00:49,275
and then the country's main
prison for another half-century.

13
00:00:49,275 --> 00:00:52,375
This is the gaol's own story.

14
00:00:52,375 --> 00:00:55,503
By the early 1930s,
Singapore's only prison,

15
00:00:55,583 --> 00:00:59,368
at Pearl's Hill — later
known as Outram Road Prison —

16
00:00:59,448 --> 00:01:04,625
was overcrowded and could not keep
different classes of prisoner apart.

17
00:01:04,625 --> 00:01:08,722
In January 1933 the Legislative
Council approved a new

18
00:01:08,802 --> 00:01:13,441
convict gaol at Changi, out on
the island's rural east coast,

19
00:01:13,521 --> 00:01:17,775
and the local firm Woh Hup
began building it that year.

20
00:01:17,775 --> 00:01:20,822
The design came from the
Public Works Department —

21
00:01:20,902 --> 00:01:24,762
an academic study of the prison's
heritage names the government

22
00:01:24,842 --> 00:01:28,264
architect Frank Dorrington
Ward as the man responsible —

23
00:01:28,344 --> 00:01:32,203
and it followed the "telephone-pole"
plan of the modern prison:

24
00:01:32,283 --> 00:01:36,518
two four-storey cell blocks branching
off a central covered corridor,

25
00:01:36,598 --> 00:01:39,475
so that warders could
reach any block quickly.

26
00:01:39,475 --> 00:01:42,773
Around the cells stood work
rooms, a hospital block,

27
00:01:42,853 --> 00:01:46,930
punishment cells, a separate small
block for European prisoners,

28
00:01:47,010 --> 00:01:51,607
and a perimeter wall six metres high
with a watchtower turret at each of

29
00:01:51,687 --> 00:01:55,650
its four corners, entered through
a double-leafed steel gate.

30
00:01:55,650 --> 00:02:00,143
It was built for 600 long-sentence
prisoners, and by the standards

31
00:02:00,223 --> 00:02:03,954
of its day it was remarkably
civilised in its fittings:

32
00:02:04,034 --> 00:02:09,982
a modern sewerage system, a central laundry
and kitchen, showers by the exercise yards,

33
00:02:10,062 --> 00:02:14,150
and in every cell an electric
light and a flushing latrine.

34
00:02:14,150 --> 00:02:18,262
Roots, the National Heritage
Board's portal, calls the finished

35
00:02:18,342 --> 00:02:22,400
prison "the most modern institution
of its kind in the East".

36
00:02:22,400 --> 00:02:26,848
It was gazetted as a prison
on the 24th of December 1936

37
00:02:26,928 --> 00:02:31,698
and received its first prisoners
on the 4th of January 1937,

38
00:02:31,778 --> 00:02:35,256
long-term men transferred
from Pearl's Hill,

39
00:02:35,336 --> 00:02:40,025
which settled down to holding
short-term convicts instead.

40
00:02:40,025 --> 00:02:44,935
One of its cell doors survives in
the National Museum of Singapore,

41
00:02:45,015 --> 00:02:49,701
a heavy blue steel door with its
inspection flap and food hatch,

42
00:02:49,781 --> 00:02:54,100
labelled simply as a Changi
Prison cell door of the 1930s.

43
00:02:54,100 --> 00:02:57,307
It is worth remembering
that door when the numbers

44
00:02:57,387 --> 00:03:00,462
start to climb, because
everything that happened

45
00:03:00,542 --> 00:03:03,500
to this building happened
one cell at a time.

46
00:03:03,500 --> 00:03:05,830
"Changi" the wartime camp was much

47
00:03:05,910 --> 00:03:08,675
bigger than Changi Prison the building.

48
00:03:08,675 --> 00:03:12,477
Most Allied prisoners of war
spent the war in and around

49
00:03:12,557 --> 00:03:16,151
the British Army's own
barracks at Changi — Selarang,

50
00:03:16,231 --> 00:03:21,419
Roberts and Kitchener — and this blog
has told two of those stories already:

51
00:03:21,499 --> 00:03:24,608
the Selarang Square
Squeeze of September 1942,

52
00:03:24,688 --> 00:03:28,500
and Stanley Warren's chapel
murals at Roberts Barracks.

53
00:03:28,500 --> 00:03:32,232
The gaol had a different
war: first it held civilians,

54
00:03:32,312 --> 00:03:35,700
and only at the end was
it filled with soldiers.

55
00:03:35,700 --> 00:03:40,300
Singapore fell on the
15th of February 1942.

56
00:03:40,300 --> 00:03:44,698
Two days later, on the 17th of
February, the colony's European

57
00:03:44,778 --> 00:03:48,750
civilians were rounded up
and marched to Changi Prison.

58
00:03:48,750 --> 00:03:53,907
The National Library Board's
breakdown of who went in

59
00:03:53,987 --> 00:03:59,341
is 2,200 men, 359 women and
61 children — 2,620 people,

60
00:03:59,421 --> 00:04:04,281
other accounts putting the
total somewhere between

61
00:04:04,361 --> 00:04:09,400
2,400 and 2,500 — into a
building designed for 600.

62
00:04:09,400 --> 00:04:13,055
Among them, in one of the
neatest reversals of the war,

63
00:04:13,135 --> 00:04:18,025
was the Governor of the Straits
Settlements himself, Sir Shenton Thomas.

64
00:04:18,025 --> 00:04:23,125
The Japanese largely left the
internees to run the place themselves.

65
00:04:23,125 --> 00:04:27,514
Each block elected its leader;
the internees maintained the camp,

66
00:04:27,594 --> 00:04:32,601
ran schools for the children (history
and geography were banned subjects),

67
00:04:32,681 --> 00:04:35,775
and organised concerts,
theatre and lectures.

68
00:04:35,775 --> 00:04:39,924
Men and women were held in separate
blocks and forbidden to meet,

69
00:04:40,004 --> 00:04:43,697
but families passed messages
to each other in code through

70
00:04:43,777 --> 00:04:47,550
the scripts and songs of the
concerts each side performed.

71
00:04:47,550 --> 00:04:52,110
The women produced a camp
newspaper, POW WOW, led by a former

72
00:04:52,190 --> 00:04:57,131
Malaya Tribune journalist, Freddy
Bloom, which ran from April 1942

73
00:04:57,211 --> 00:05:01,775
for almost nineteen months; the
men had the Changi Guardian.

74
00:05:01,775 --> 00:05:06,192
Food in the first year was adequate
if basic, and the building's

75
00:05:06,272 --> 00:05:11,250
modern plumbing spared the internees
some of the worst of camp disease —

76
00:05:11,330 --> 00:05:15,888
but dysentery and malaria were
constant, and as the war ground on,

77
00:05:15,968 --> 00:05:20,675
malnutrition diseases such as
beri-beri and tuberculosis took hold.

78
00:05:20,675 --> 00:05:24,202
In 1942, mainly between March and August,

79
00:05:24,282 --> 00:05:27,625
the women internees made three quilts.

80
00:05:27,625 --> 00:05:31,633
Each is a signature quilt
of 66 embroidered squares,

81
00:05:31,713 --> 00:05:35,250
every square signed by
the woman who made it.

82
00:05:35,250 --> 00:05:39,479
The idea came from Ethel
Mulvany, a Canadian internee who

83
00:05:39,559 --> 00:05:43,410
had been a Red Cross
representative in Singapore and

84
00:05:43,490 --> 00:05:47,875
acted as the women's Red Cross
representative in the camp.

85
00:05:47,875 --> 00:05:51,801
The quilts were partly to relieve
boredom and raise morale —

86
00:05:51,881 --> 00:05:55,006
but their real purpose
was to let the men in the

87
00:05:55,086 --> 00:05:58,825
other camps know that the
women and children were alive.

88
00:05:58,825 --> 00:06:01,441
Each woman was asked to put something of

89
00:06:01,521 --> 00:06:04,150
herself into her square, with her name.

90
00:06:04,150 --> 00:06:07,662
Mulvany won the Japanese
commandant's permission by

91
00:06:07,742 --> 00:06:10,973
presenting the quilts as
gifts for the wounded,

92
00:06:11,053 --> 00:06:15,350
and by making one of the three
for wounded Japanese soldiers.

93
00:06:15,350 --> 00:06:18,471
Even then, the word
"prison" was not acceptable

94
00:06:18,551 --> 00:06:22,763
and had to be unpicked from the
squares as they were assembled;

95
00:06:22,843 --> 00:06:24,750
the word "gaol" was allowed.

96
00:06:24,750 --> 00:06:28,493
The Australian War Memorial,
which now holds the Australian

97
00:06:28,573 --> 00:06:31,799
and Japanese quilts — the
British quilt is with the

98
00:06:31,879 --> 00:06:36,205
British Red Cross in London — is
careful about what the quilts were:

99
00:06:36,285 --> 00:06:39,834
there is no evidence they
were ever used by the wounded,

100
00:06:39,914 --> 00:06:43,025
and they were not made
as objects of resistance.

101
00:06:43,025 --> 00:06:47,587
They were, more simply and more
movingly, a list of names in thread,

102
00:06:47,667 --> 00:06:50,875
sent out of the wire in
the hope of being read.

103
00:06:50,875 --> 00:06:53,902
No photograph of them is freely licensed,

104
00:06:53,982 --> 00:06:58,075
so on this blog, as in the
camp, they travel in words.

105
00:06:58,075 --> 00:07:01,978
On the night of 26–the
27th of September 1943,

106
00:07:02,058 --> 00:07:06,912
Australian commandos of
Operation Jaywick sank or damaged

107
00:07:06,992 --> 00:07:10,975
seven Japanese vessels
in Singapore's harbour.

108
00:07:10,975 --> 00:07:15,548
The Kempeitai, the Japanese
military police, were convinced the

109
00:07:15,628 --> 00:07:20,725
attack had been directed by a spy
network among the Changi internees.

110
00:07:20,725 --> 00:07:24,253
On the 10th of October
1943 — the Double Tenth,

111
00:07:24,333 --> 00:07:28,553
the tenth day of the tenth
month — they raided the gaol,

112
00:07:28,633 --> 00:07:32,625
and over the following
months arrested 57 internees.

113
00:07:32,625 --> 00:07:37,355
The 57 were taken not back to
cells but to the YMCA building

114
00:07:37,435 --> 00:07:43,447
on Orchard Road, the Central Police
Station and Smith Street Police Station,

115
00:07:43,527 --> 00:07:47,375
and interrogated under
torture until April 1944.

116
00:07:47,375 --> 00:07:49,300
Fifteen died.

117
00:07:49,300 --> 00:07:54,250
One more, an ambulance driver
named John Long, was executed.

118
00:07:54,250 --> 00:07:58,036
Among the tortured were the
Anglican Bishop of Singapore,

119
00:07:58,116 --> 00:08:02,174
John Leonard Wilson, and, in
the wider sweep, Elizabeth Choy,

120
00:08:02,254 --> 00:08:08,075
who had run a canteen at Tan Tock Seng Hospital
and passed food and money to prisoners;

121
00:08:08,155 --> 00:08:11,275
she survived nearly 200
days of interrogation.

122
00:08:11,275 --> 00:08:15,638
In May 1944 the surviving
civilians were moved out of the

123
00:08:15,718 --> 00:08:20,550
gaol to Sime Road Camp, where
they stayed until the war ended.

124
00:08:20,550 --> 00:08:25,275
The civilians' departure made
room for a grimmer population.

125
00:08:25,275 --> 00:08:30,155
In May 1944 the Japanese
concentrated the Allied prisoners

126
00:08:30,235 --> 00:08:34,425
of war of the Changi area
in and around the gaol.

127
00:08:34,425 --> 00:08:38,303
The Australian War Memorial's
figures give the shape of it:

128
00:08:38,383 --> 00:08:42,195
about 5,000 men squeezed into
the prison building itself —

129
00:08:42,275 --> 00:08:46,757
five men to a cell built for one —
and some 11,700 prisoners in all,

130
00:08:46,837 --> 00:08:50,447
in less than a quarter of a
square kilometre around it,

131
00:08:50,527 --> 00:08:54,338
with attap huts put up in the
courtyards for the overflow;

132
00:08:54,418 --> 00:08:59,450
the National Library Board rounds the
complex's population to about 12,000.

133
00:08:59,450 --> 00:09:03,356
Conditions in the gaol were
worse than in the barracks

134
00:09:03,436 --> 00:09:06,234
the men had come from: tighter control,

135
00:09:06,314 --> 00:09:11,253
shorter rations, and in July 1944
the Japanese revoked the authority

136
00:09:11,333 --> 00:09:14,950
of the Allied senior
officers over their own men.

137
00:09:14,950 --> 00:09:18,580
Even so, the prisoners opened a makeshift

138
00:09:18,660 --> 00:09:22,550
Playhouse Theatre inside
in September 1944.

139
00:09:22,550 --> 00:09:26,663
By the last months of the war,
food was so short that men were

140
00:09:26,743 --> 00:09:30,800
eating sparrows and rats to
supplement what they were given.

141
00:09:30,800 --> 00:09:34,252
Japan surrendered on the
2nd of September 1945,

142
00:09:34,332 --> 00:09:39,212
and the prisoners at Changi were
released on the 6th of September,

143
00:09:39,292 --> 00:09:43,650
six days before the formal
surrender ceremony in the city.

144
00:09:43,650 --> 00:09:47,968
The photograph at the head of this
post — men pouring out of a gate,

145
00:09:48,048 --> 00:09:51,800
waving, half of them in nothing
but shorts — is that week.

146
00:09:51,800 --> 00:09:54,725
The gaol then changed sides.

147
00:09:54,725 --> 00:09:58,591
It now held Japanese war-crimes
suspects, and it became the

148
00:09:58,671 --> 00:10:02,738
place where Singapore's war-crimes
sentences were carried out:

149
00:10:02,818 --> 00:10:06,148
the National Library Board
records that most of the

150
00:10:06,228 --> 00:10:09,760
executions for those convicted
with a death sentence —

151
00:10:09,840 --> 00:10:13,305
by hanging or by firing
squad — took place at Changi,

152
00:10:13,385 --> 00:10:18,200
and the newspapers of 1946 and 1947
carry the brief reports to prove it.

153
00:10:18,200 --> 00:10:20,895
One published count puts the

154
00:10:20,975 --> 00:10:23,850
number executed there at 135.

155
00:10:23,850 --> 00:10:27,983
In all, 131 British
military trials were held

156
00:10:28,063 --> 00:10:32,570
in Singapore between
January 1946 and March 1948,

157
00:10:32,650 --> 00:10:36,675
most of them in the
Supreme Court Building.

158
00:10:36,675 --> 00:10:42,743
The trial with the most direct line back
to the gaol was the Double Tenth trial,

159
00:10:42,823 --> 00:10:47,050
heard from the 18th of March
to the 15th of April 1946.

160
00:10:47,050 --> 00:10:50,916
Twenty-one Kempeitai
officers and interpreters

161
00:10:50,996 --> 00:10:55,200
stood accused of the
torture of the 57 internees.

162
00:10:55,200 --> 00:11:00,268
Fourteen were found guilty; eight,
including the Singapore Kempeitai commander,

163
00:11:00,348 --> 00:11:02,418
Lieutenant Colonel Sumida Haruzo,

164
00:11:02,498 --> 00:11:06,849
were sentenced to death by hanging,
and three to life imprisonment —

165
00:11:06,929 --> 00:11:11,280
though two of the condemned,
interpreters who were British subjects,

166
00:11:11,360 --> 00:11:15,400
were later retried in the local
courts and imprisoned instead.

167
00:11:15,400 --> 00:11:19,744
On the 15th of October 1947
the prison was handed back

168
00:11:19,824 --> 00:11:24,414
to the Prisons Department,
and the long-sentence men from

169
00:11:24,494 --> 00:11:29,000
Pearl's Hill came back to
fill it, as they had in 1937.

170
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:33,779
For the next half-century Changi
was simply Singapore's main prison,

171
00:11:33,859 --> 00:11:35,779
and its peacetime history is

172
00:11:35,859 --> 00:11:39,575
quieter and stranger than
its wartime fame suggests.

173
00:11:39,575 --> 00:11:44,568
It was the first prison in Singapore
with land enough for a farm:

174
00:11:44,648 --> 00:11:48,471
from July 1949 a 30-acre
prisoner-run farm outside

175
00:11:48,551 --> 00:11:52,451
the walls grew vegetables
for the prisoners' meals,

176
00:11:52,531 --> 00:11:57,290
followed by pigs in 1950, chickens
in 1951 and cattle in 1953,

177
00:11:57,370 --> 00:12:01,975
until the prison was largely
self-sufficient in fresh food.

178
00:12:01,975 --> 00:12:05,871
Good-conduct prisoners worked
the farm under light supervision,

179
00:12:05,951 --> 00:12:07,764
and though there were escapes,

180
00:12:07,844 --> 00:12:11,125
the authorities kept it for
what it did for the men.

181
00:12:11,125 --> 00:12:15,439
A graded earning scheme for
prison labour followed in 1950,

182
00:12:15,519 --> 00:12:18,200
paying eight to fifteen cents a day.

183
00:12:18,200 --> 00:12:22,995
In 1955 Changi introduced corrective
training for young offenders

184
00:12:23,075 --> 00:12:26,595
and preventive detention
for older recidivists —

185
00:12:26,675 --> 00:12:32,820
Singapore reportedly the first place in
Southeast Asia to do so — and from 1960 the

186
00:12:32,900 --> 00:12:37,775
National Library supplied the
prison with books in all languages.

187
00:12:37,775 --> 00:12:41,963
A Prisons Department
report of 1948 had counted

188
00:12:42,043 --> 00:12:48,230
597 cells and reckoned the gaol
could hold 1,000 inmates comfortably,

189
00:12:48,310 --> 00:12:51,125
a long way from five to a cell.

190
00:12:51,125 --> 00:12:55,729
Between the late 1970s and
the early 1980s the prison was

191
00:12:55,809 --> 00:13:01,150
upgraded with two new five-storey
blocks to relieve overcrowding.

192
00:13:01,150 --> 00:13:05,875
The gaol also held the political
prisoners of the new nation.

193
00:13:05,875 --> 00:13:10,126
Trade unionists and suspected
communists were detained there

194
00:13:10,206 --> 00:13:14,169
through the 1950s and 1960s,
among them many swept up in

195
00:13:14,249 --> 00:13:18,644
Operation Cold Store in 1963 —
Lim Chin Siong, Fong Swee Suan,

196
00:13:18,724 --> 00:13:23,120
Lim Hock Siew, James Puthucheary,
Poh Soo Kai, Said Zahari and

197
00:13:23,200 --> 00:13:27,675
Devan Nair among the names the
National Library Board records.

198
00:13:27,675 --> 00:13:32,124
Nair and others arrested in
the unrest of October 1956 were

199
00:13:32,204 --> 00:13:36,807
released from Changi on the 4th
of June 1959, greeted by more

200
00:13:36,887 --> 00:13:41,414
than 2,000 supporters, after
Lee Kuan Yew made their release

201
00:13:41,494 --> 00:13:46,100
a condition of forming Singapore's
first elected government.

202
00:13:46,100 --> 00:13:49,535
And through all these
decades, hanging remained the

203
00:13:49,615 --> 00:13:52,705
gaol's other function:
capital punishment was,

204
00:13:52,785 --> 00:13:56,575
and in the new complex still
is, carried out at Changi.

205
00:13:56,575 --> 00:14:01,419
By the late 1990s the government
had decided to gather Singapore's

206
00:14:01,499 --> 00:14:06,050
scattered prisons into one modern
complex on the Changi site.

207
00:14:06,050 --> 00:14:09,526
Ground was broken for the new 48-hectare

208
00:14:09,606 --> 00:14:14,148
Changi Prison Complex on
the 31st of December 1999 —

209
00:14:14,228 --> 00:14:17,250
and the old gaol stood in its way.

210
00:14:17,250 --> 00:14:21,788
The plan to demolish it drew
resistance from heritage advocates,

211
00:14:21,868 --> 00:14:25,468
former prisoners of war
and Australian politicians,

212
00:14:25,548 --> 00:14:29,950
for whom the building was a war
memorial as much as a prison.

213
00:14:29,950 --> 00:14:34,121
The compromise, announced in
March 2004, was a fragment:

214
00:14:34,201 --> 00:14:38,296
the entrance gate, a 180-metre
stretch of the perimeter

215
00:14:38,376 --> 00:14:42,475
wall and two of the four
corner turrets would be kept.

216
00:14:42,475 --> 00:14:45,553
Everything else — the
cell blocks, the hospital,

217
00:14:45,633 --> 00:14:48,725
the yards, the farm —
was demolished that year.

218
00:14:48,725 --> 00:14:52,963
In August 2004 the new complex's
Cluster A was unveiled,

219
00:14:53,043 --> 00:14:56,433
and the old prison's
inmates were transferred

220
00:14:56,513 --> 00:15:00,600
into it along with those of
three other institutions.

221
00:15:00,600 --> 00:15:04,229
A second cluster opened in January 2010,

222
00:15:04,309 --> 00:15:08,575
and the complex can now
hold 11,000 prisoners.

223
00:15:08,575 --> 00:15:12,435
On the 15th of February
2016 — chosen deliberately,

224
00:15:12,515 --> 00:15:16,066
the 74th anniversary of
the fall of Singapore —

225
00:15:16,146 --> 00:15:21,396
the National Heritage Board gazetted
the Changi Prison Entrance Gate,

226
00:15:21,476 --> 00:15:25,725
Wall and Turrets as Singapore's
72nd National Monument.

227
00:15:25,725 --> 00:15:31,380
Announcing it, the Board's Director of
Preservation of Sites and Monuments,

228
00:15:31,460 --> 00:15:34,821
Jean Wee, said the prison
"stands today as an

229
00:15:34,901 --> 00:15:39,714
enduring symbol of the suffering
of those who defended Singapore

230
00:15:39,794 --> 00:15:44,000
during the tumultuous war
years between 1942 and 1945".

231
00:15:44,000 --> 00:15:49,038
The monument stands inside a restricted
area and is not open to the public;

232
00:15:49,118 --> 00:15:52,450
there is an information
board on Upper Changi Road

233
00:15:52,530 --> 00:15:56,625
North for anyone who goes to
look at the wall from the road.

234
00:15:56,625 --> 00:16:00,531
Singapore demolished almost all
of its most famous prison and

235
00:16:00,611 --> 00:16:04,974
kept the one part every prisoner
passed through and none could pass:

236
00:16:05,054 --> 00:16:08,975
the gate, a stretch of wall,
and the towers that watched it.

237
00:16:08,975 --> 00:16:11,661
The gaol is gone; the threshold remains,

238
00:16:11,741 --> 00:16:15,325
still guarded, still closed
— a lesser-known monument

239
00:16:15,405 --> 00:16:19,000
precisely because it can
only be read from the road.
